   "May that infinite Goodness of God be forever blessed, who is never sated with doing us good. I wrote to Your Paternity that I had placed myself in the hands of God, already sure of His divine will, which calls me to Japan by so many signs, and because Your Paternity, who stands in His place for me, has so interpreted it for me. Let all of hell now take arms — O, in what good hands do I find myself! He will know well how to keep me and make use of me in His time. Nor do I believe that my sins can stand against that incomprehensible Mercy, nor against the prayers of Your Paternity joined with those of the most precious Mother, of our Reverend Fathers Ignatius and Xavier, and of so many saints, who I believe stand ever helping me. I did not trouble to ask Your Paternity to call me to Rome, because I do not think I stand less at your feet — with the confidence I owe to so many Fathers — than if I were in Rome bodily present. Yet Your Paternity well knows that the devil on such occasions does not fail to make his effort to disturb everything; and God wills that we too labor with His grace in what we can. I write therefore to Your Paternity lest I have to hear of some disturbance; for affection makes me tremble where there is no cause to doubt. I have breathed no word to my relatives. Now I understand that they know it — I know not from whom among Ours (and this because the Father Rector published it, whence many knew it before me). My father has written me nothing of it. I do not know whether some plot is intended. My Father: I have no other Father but God and Your Paternity, to whom I have given myself wholly; guard me then, for love of that most precious blood of Christ, to the end with your prudence — not because I doubt, but because prayers are necessary when the devil puts himself in the middle. Carlo Camassa, who was my father, I have already left, with the whole world. Nothing more is needed there. He has no need of me at all. He has a thousand helps where there is no need. He has the means to live. He has well provided for the daughters; there remains to him in the world a son already grown and married; another in the Society with Father Giovanni Filippo Camassa, who is his brother, to close his eyes — and I am his no longer. I am God’s, of that most holy blood of Jesus Christ, and of so many poor little souls, delivered to Your Paternity as most unworthy son, in whose hands and at whose feet (so I feel impelled) I renew and make the vow of Obedience — particularly to go, where I am sent, to Japan (if it shall please you to accept it) — of Poverty and perpetual Chastity; and to enter this most holy Society of Jesus to live and die in it, offering myself wholly as a holocaust to the grace of God, although most unworthy, yet confiding in the most precious blood of Jesus Christ. And so I finish, no longer with fear, but cheerful and secure. I remain at the feet of Your Paternity, begging you in conclusion to obtain for me from the Lord heart and spirit to correspond to so great a Goodness of God. And forgive me the length."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Diego Camassa. From Naples, June 16, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Camassa, Diego",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, June 16, 1617",
  "date_only": "June 16, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 16,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 51,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2804"
 },
 "35": {
  "number": 35,
  "byline_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio — Parma, October 6, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 242",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "What I once wrote to Your Paternity, I now write to Your Paternity once again by the will of the Holy Spirit, and I shall never cease until Your Paternity has granted me what I ask. Behold, then, I open to Your Paternity the secrets which God has rooted in my heart, and into your most benign hands I consign them with all my affection — not as one utters what one would rather not say, but as one speaks to a most lovable Shepherd, as Your Paternity is: something I have to scatter throughout the whole universe of the world, better than Triptolemus ever scattered the grain with his right hand. But to open to Your Paternity what I wish, I say that I desire to go to China (as I have already asked), because I know for certain that this is the will of God, He having revealed it to me in prayer; and also because I feel, and continually experience, that I have always been inclined thither, both in the world and much more in Religion. Now it is for Your Paternity to execute what God has set in motion, so that I may render to God that hundredfold which He requires of me; but if by chance Your Paternity should deny it me, I am of the opinion that everything will turn out for me topsyturvy. Let not Your Paternity say what is going about — that I am increasing the pot before the lentils — for the spirit of God goes where it pleases; he is not a weathercock, as they say, but brings to completion the things begun and promoted by him. From this, then, let Your Paternity know my desire, which is to go find Father Nicolò Trigault before he departs further; whom having found, I shall be able with tranquil mind to attend to the study of philosophy. Let Your Paternity now take care not to contradict itself, exhorting us with such grave words to so great a work, while denying me this. Already Your Paternity has heard that messis quidem multa, operarii autem pauci (the harvest indeed is great, but the laborers are few); therefore with a generous hand let Your Paternity provide for it, and I, although unworthy, would be one of those. Therefore I pray you, by the most sacred bowels of the mercy of God, with which coming from heaven He visited us, and by His most holy passion, that as soon as possible Your Paternity send me. Be well."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your most humble brother in Christ — Antonio Rondoni. From Parma, October 6, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Parma",
  "date_raw": "Parma, October 6, 1617",
  "date_only": "October 6, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 6,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 53,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2774"
 },
 "36": {
  "number": 36,
  "byline_raw": "Barletta, Francesco — Naples, October 16, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 243",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "With the opportunity of the coming to Rome of Father Francesco Albertino, of whom I have until now been a companion, I could not refrain from writing four lines, showing you the great affliction my heart feels; for hoping to be consoled by Your Paternity together with those Fathers who are now going to the Indies, you reply to me in your last letter that you are writing to the Father Rector that he write me in the catalogue of those who have the vocation. My Father, I desire something other than to be written down. My desire is to go to the Indies, and to be written down as one who has the vocation for the Indies; therefore if you desire to console me, as the most loving Father that you are, accompany me with those who are going to the Indies, and do not have me written among those who have the vocation for the Indies; and believe me that my soul will never be consoled until it obtain that which it so longs for: which is, in the midst of fierce and barbarous people, to depart, to be mistreated, and to be martyred for Christ. My Father, let not Your Paternity despise the desire that God has given me; but rather, as His vicar, execute it as soon as possible — nothing else. I commend myself to your most holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most obedient servant in Christ — Francesco Barletta. From Naples, October 16, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Barletta, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, October 16, 1617",
  "date_only": "October 16, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 16,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 55,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2768"
 },
 "37": {
  "number": 37,
  "byline_raw": "Camassa, Diego — Naples, October 24, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 249",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I have learned from the letter of the Father Assistant of Portugal that Your Paternity, for I know not what news from Japan, wishes to defer our departure to next year, when we should have arrived to do some good. God knows how I have felt it on the one hand. It was not enough for the devil that he would have driven me from Japan, unless he also confines us in Italy, shut up as in a narrow prison. And what good is it that the Lord has made me overcome so many difficulties, since when I thought to enjoy the grace so long awaited, and now received, I see myself so far from it! My father sought nothing else, that meanwhile some other impediment might arise. But I hope, by the infinite goodness of God, it will not be so, everything being ordered by the most sweet hand of His divine providence through Your Paternity. This alone certainly makes me, instead of sighing continually, remain secure and cheerful; and I console myself that we are not the masters of God’s glory. Enough. That most precious blood of the Crucified will bring everything to its end, for He began it, since the cause is His, not ours. So I thank Your Paternity, who has given me the opportunity to make a sacrifice to His Divine Majesty; and certainly it is no small sacrifice for me. But I pray Your Paternity that, if the difficulties are surmountable, you not leave us, for the love of God, any longer disconsolate in this matter; for we are still in time. I would ask that Your Paternity call us meanwhile to Rome, so that I may stand more secure at your feet (for it seems to me that business is handled differently in person than by letters; and indeed those who were in Rome have departed — blessed be God). But nescio quid petam (I know not what to ask) — I know not what I ask. Your Paternity wishes me more good than I can imagine. If you assure me that staying in Naples does not make me more distant from Your Paternity than I would be in Rome, this too can be offered to the Lord. My Father, I stand clean in your hands: make and unmake of me as you please, provided that with your holy Sacrifices and prayers you keep me within the wounds of the Crucified, so that before going to Japan I may learn to navigate that immense ocean of the charity of God."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Diego Camassa. From Naples, October 24, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Camassa, Diego",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, October 24, 1617",
  "date_only": "October 24, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 24,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 56,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2805"
 },
 "38": {
  "number": 38,
  "byline_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio — Parma, November 26, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 262",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I remain under the greatest obligation to Your Paternity for the remembrance lately sent me; from which I truly discern how much affection you bear your subjects. From this love springs the fact that all things are so well ordered. But by this convenient occasion, I was pleased to let Your Paternity know that it was not by the revelation that I was moved to this enterprise, but because God has so willed, giving me a prompt will; and for this I have armed my breast with a strong cuirass, to be able to bear all the adversities that will oppose the attainment of this my (or rather God’s) intention. And if the young plant is still too weak to bear so great a weight, God will strengthen it, and with His grace will fence it about, so that it may not be moved from the work begun. I come, then, once more to Your Paternity, and pray you to deign to grant me this grace, so that I may labor in the new vineyard of the Lord, since He continually urges me on with fresh and vigorous goads."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant — Antonio Rondonio. From Parma, November 26, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Parma",
  "date_raw": "Parma, November 26, 1617",
  "date_only": "November 26, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 11,
  "day": 26,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 58,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2775"
 },
 "39": {
  "number": 39,
  "byline_raw": "Balami, Ignazio — Palermo, December 31, 1617",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 273",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Desirous of seeing myself employed in the help of souls in the most extreme parts of the world, that I may in some way begin to show myself grateful for the mercy shown me by my Jesus, I come with this letter to pray Your Paternity to deign to remember me and to expose me in whatever place shall please you, as I have written at other times; and I tell you in truth, Father General, that the Lord has so penetrated my soul with this affection that a thousand years seems to me every hour; indeed I take consolation in nothing else but in thinking that Your Paternity, guided by the Holy Spirit, will soon console me. For when I think of my sins, the business of my salvation seems to me a very difficult thing, if I do not come to shed my blood for my Jesus, or at least if I do not expose myself to this. So, to trouble you no more, with tears in my eyes I pray Your Paternity to console me, and not to regard my weakness of spirit and ability; for I hope that the Lord who calls me and gives me this affection and desire will give me such strength of spirit that I may serve Him as I ought, to show myself grateful. Again, for the sake of the mysteries of the mercy of Jesus Christ, on my knees and weeping, I commend to you the business of my salvation; and let Your Paternity believe me, Father General, that I feel my strength failing me — consolation, consolation! I do not cease, nor shall I cease, unworthily though it be, to pray the Lord for the health of Your Paternity, that the Society may long enjoy such a Father. Humbly I ask your blessing, persuading myself that I shall soon be consoled."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, poor servant and unworthy son — Ignatio Balami. From Palermo, December 31, 1617."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Balami, Ignazio",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, December 31, 1617",
  "date_only": "December 31, 1617",
  "year": 1617,
  "month": 12,
  "day": 31,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 59,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2831"
 },
 "40": {
  "number": 40,
  "byline_raw": "Abellone, Hermete — Genoa, January 2, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 1",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "So ardent is the desire which almighty God gives me to suffer something for His love, that I would greatly fear not to correspond to His loving invitation if I did not make it known to Your Reverence, as to the one who is to accept the invitation — just as some great personage would do, to whom some great King asked for his youngest child, to clothe him in the same livery and garments with which his own son was clothed while he lived in this life, and to treat him in the same manner of life in which he was treated. I say, then, to Your Reverence that I am invited by that supreme King to the same table and to the same manner of life at which His blessed Son fed and in which He lived — that is, to be called to go to the Indies or to some other place where there is hope of suffering great things for His love, even to the shedding of my very blood. Ah, accept, I pray you, accept, Your Reverence, so loving an invitation; and do not refuse, seeing above all how majestic and loving is the Inviter, who is the omnipotent and benign God, and how vile and discourteous is the invited, who is the abominable and ungrateful sinner, Hermete Abellone — with which, in holy indifference, I give myself wholly to Your Reverence, and pray you to deign to commend me to the Lord in your holy sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Reverence, unworthy son in Christ — Hermete Abellone, novice of the Society of Jesus. From Genoa, January 2, in the year of the Lord 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Abellone, Hermete",
  "place_raw": "Genoa",
  "date_raw": "Genoa, January 2, 1618",
  "date_only": "January 2, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 2,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 61,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2735"
 },
 "41": {
  "number": 41,
  "byline_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo — Palermo, February 9, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 12",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Many are the reasons and motives that impel me to write and to remind Your Paternity of my desire for the Indies: for if I turn toward the East, I see the blood shed by the holy Christians, which, like seed, promises most beautiful shoots and great fruit of souls, and consequently demands cultivators and laborers of that blessed land, that the shoot, being cultivated, may be preserved, and come at last to perfection, and bear that fruit which our Lord God expects of us; and if this should not come to pass, I stand in great fear lest He command that we not set sail, and that those souls, left to themselves, hurl themselves headlong into the infernal abyss. And this fruit has at times not been borne for lack of care and cultivation — wherefore it is mine to offer myself to Your Paternity and to pray you instantly that you may deign to grant me to serve the Lord; the voice calls me.",
   "On the other hand, I feel myself drawn by the example of our brothers, who with such charity and love toward our Lord God, and from their great desire to convert souls, did not hesitate to expose themselves to death for the holy faith, and indeed left their lives there. Would to God that I might one day be their humble imitator; and if I should not be worthy of this, at least let not Your Paternity deny my going to those parts where this desire may easily be put into execution — for I shall live content with the mere hope that the same might befall me, if my defects do not impede it. I close, commending myself to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most humble servant and son in Christ — Vincenzo Fassari. From Palermo, February 9, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, February 9, 1618",
  "date_only": "February 9, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 62,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2819"
 },
 "42": {
  "number": 42,
  "byline_raw": "Camassa, Diego — Naples, April 6, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 24",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I write this to Your Paternity, so as not to lose the good occasion of these holy days to give a thousand good holiday greetings to Your Paternity — indeed to myself — and I have awaited it these many months for no small interest of my own. Believe me, my Father, that since October, since I have not written to Your Paternity, the hours seem years to me until I arrive at my Japan; nor have I had any other consolation, truly, in this time, than the sure hope in the determination of the Lord, upon the word of Your Paternity, who with your charity has opened to me your heart forever. You have promised me a good that is like a non plus ultra (no further beyond) of what I could desire in this life.",
   "I am at peace, my Father, and I know that Your Paternity, imitating the loving bowels of blessed Christ Crucified, who knows how to do nothing but good, even to one who deserves nothing but every evil — I know that you love me, oh, how much more than I merit. May the Lord and the Most Holy Mother render to you for me, in that eminent manner of yours, what I cannot.",
   "But if anything can be added to the charity Your Paternity shows me, give me leave to ask of you one thing for its completion: that Your Paternity, not forgetting our exile, for the love of God call us quickly to Rome at the beginning of September, for it will be a most particular consolation both for Brother Girolamo and for me, to find ourselves soon in Rome, to have time to visit those holy places, and, if it shall so please, to wander as pilgrims to the Holy House, besides placing ourselves in greater safety — for one who loves, my Father, cannot see the hour, and must of necessity fear.",
   "With this promise, I would desire Your Paternity to give me the Easter good wishes. And I meanwhile do not cease to pray in my cold prayers for the health of Your Paternity. May the Lord grant to Your Paternity every day a greater abundance of His treasures, for the universal good, and for those poor Japanese souls, and for the Society, and for the whole Church. And, making you most humble reverence, I commend myself to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity servant and most unworthy son in the Lord — Diego Camassa. From Naples, April 6, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Camassa, Diego",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, April 6, 1618",
  "date_only": "April 6, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 6,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 64,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2800"
 },
 "43": {
  "number": 43,
  "byline_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio — Parma, May 18, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 36",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Your Paternity will forgive me if, by my frequent writing, I come to annoy you: it is God who moves me to do this; and although I am that useless servant I have always been, nonetheless, since the vocations and offices which His Divine Majesty is wont to give to men are diverse, the same, by His divine grace, gives the fitting aids and forces.",
   "Wherefore, since God has called me to the salvation of souls, whether in China, or Japan, or Brazil, etc., although unworthy of such a vocation and unfit for this office, nonetheless He, who always gives grace in conformity with the office, will give me fitting forces. Therefore may Your Paternity, placed by God in such rank that you may govern His Religion, for love of Him, and for His greater honor, and the salvation of my soul, grant me leave for the next convenient occasion of the Father Procurator of Brazil (who, as we have been told, is in Rome), that I may go with him.",
   "I know full well my few and slight forces; but because God, with particular movement, has moved me to ask again for this of Your Paternity, I have written. I would not wish to have written for ceremony, nor indeed do I truly write, but that God may be the more glorified, our Society served, and the souls to whose salvation I shall be employed, and also my own, may remain saved, obtaining from Your Paternity what I ask.",
   "I shall not offer to Your Paternity, for this voyage, any instruments — that is, money and other useful things like books, etc. — being able, after I have had the license, to find them more freely, if it shall so please Your Paternity. But do not abandon me: give me what I ask; for if it be needful to ask it a thousand times and more, I shall always do so, that God may thus be glorified — nothing else. Not to weary you further: Of Your Paternity —"
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most unworthy servant — Antonio Rondonio. From Parma, May 18, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Parma",
  "date_raw": "Parma, May 18, 1618",
  "date_only": "May 18, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 18,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 66,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2769"
 },
 "44": {
  "number": 44,
  "byline_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo — Palermo, May 25, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 44",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "As the Lord urges me with continual desires that I ask of Your Paternity to go to the Indies, I am compelled to put it into execution by means of letters, since in no other way can I do it. And I do it now the more willingly, inasmuch as it seems to me the time is near when the Lord will grant me this grace, by His infinite goodness and mercy; for since He has given me the desire, He will also give me the accomplishing of it; and what gladdens me most is that He will not delay to console me — which in the second place I expect from Your Paternity, to whom it has been committed by God to deny or to grant me this grace.",
   "I ask nothing else of him but to be able to go and to suffer something for the love of Christ Jesus, and to employ myself wholly in the aid of souls redeemed with the price of His divine Blood — this being the institute to which the Lord has called me; and although this exercise is found also in these parts, yet in my ears resound the tearful voices of those poor gentiles who ask for salvation, and perhaps there lacks one to bring it to them. And it moves me all the more that, without thought of this, they continually descend to hell without other remedy.",
   "I pray therefore Your Paternity that, among the others you will send for the aid of those peoples, you may number me, though unworthy and unfit; for I hope in the Lord that He will give me all that is needful for such a mission. For the rest, I commend myself to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most humble servant in Christ — Vincenzo Fassari. From Palermo, May 25, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, May 25, 1618",
  "date_only": "May 25, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 25,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 68,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2820"
 },
 "45": {
  "number": 45,
  "byline_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio — Mantua, June 27, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 53",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I write anew to Your Paternity to show him my desire. Your Paternity will forgive me if I annoy him with my letters; but because I am always moved by the desire to aid souls — that are throughout the whole world, but, by particular grace of God, those that are either in the Indies, in China, or in Brazil and other like places — therefore anew I ask him, and pray him, by that Divine Majesty, that, by the good occasion of the Father who goes to Brazil, he may be content to give me leave, that I may be able to go with him.",
   "In the last letter I wrote to Your Paternity, I promised him good things for this voyage; now anew I promise them to him, which I shall find, once he has given me the license. This is what I have to write, and I shall not lengthen my letter further, lest I annoy Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most unworthy servant — Antonio Rondonio. From Mantua (where I was sent by the Father Provincial to teach the humanities), June 27, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Mantua",
  "date_raw": "Mantua, June 27, 1618",
  "date_only": "June 27, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 27,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 70,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2770"
 },
 "46": {
  "number": 46,
  "byline_raw": "Barletta, Francesco — Naples, July 17, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 60",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I have had good news from Your Paternity by means of Father […] d'Amici and Brother Ottavio Messi — although I expected better than that, in conformity with my desire. And it is that Your Paternity is content to do me the charity that I come thither, and then go to visit the Holy House of Loreto. The better news concerned the affair of the Indies, and truly this time I expected to be told, and to accompany those two brothers who will go to Japan; and as Your Paternity had more than once promised me, I hoped that this time the promises and the desires should have effect.",
   "But I hope in our Blessed Father, in the Most Blessed Virgin, and in Saint Anne, whom I have taken as Advocate, that Your Paternity will not delay long to console me also in this — which is what I most desire; and as now you do me the grace of sending me to that House where the Son of God became incarnate, so I hope you will do me the grace of sending me to a place where I may die for the same Son of God and for the confession of His Faith.",
   "Now, Our Father, I would desire this grace as a toast to the good news that comes from Japan, of the return of Our men to that Church of Japan; for, to speak with some simplicity of mine, even I have tried to placate our Lord, seeing that a year ago I fasted for this purpose on Fridays and Saturdays, besides other things.",
   "In conclusion, you will give me leave that I, your least son, may continue to supplicate my most loving Father, reminding him that, as our Lord has given me the desire to conform myself to the will of Your Paternity and of the General Congregation regarding the cap — for since the decree made here was understood, I, with the license of the Father Provincial, ceased to wear it, and even until this time I go without it, and God knows with how many contradictions — so I would desire that Your Paternity might incline to my desires, which are inspired by God, regarding this: to send me as soon as possible to the Indies, or another place of infidels, this September. Or if Your Paternity should send me as a slave with the iron at my neck, or grant me this blessed lot of being sold for Christ, though my life is worth little or nothing.",
   "I know that I speak to a Father, and a Father who tenderly loves all his children, though unworthy; for this I add nothing more. I speak more with the heart, and I shall always speak, though with the pen, lest I weary you so. Pardon the excessive boldness of having been so long. The Father covers the defects of children, and of needy children, such as I am. May our Lord give to Your Paternity the completion and the crown of your holy labors, and of the vigilance you have for the good of your subjects."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most unworthy servant, and most humble son in Christ — Francesco Barletta. Naples, July 17, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Barletta, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, July 17, 1618",
  "date_only": "July 17, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 17,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 71,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2766"
 },
 "47": {
  "number": 47,
  "byline_raw": "Gargano, Francesco — Nola, July 25, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 63",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Although I am most certain, as indeed I have known, that Your Paternity, by your charity, keeps fresh memory of me — for which it would not be so necessary to annoy you with my letters — nevertheless I do not seem fully to satisfy myself unless I seek, by letters, not being able otherwise, the execution of what I supremely desire in this life. All the more so as that first ardent desire has not at all lessened; indeed I believe I may say it, and with truth certainly, that now it burns more and urges me on.",
   "Nor, by His infinite goodness, does blessed God fail continually to kindle it with the wind of His Mercy, and to dispose me with a certain hope — of bearing cheerfully both the torrid zones and the perils of the ocean, the furies of heaven, the tempests of the sea, the journeys by land, hunger, thirst, perils, labors, and whatever inconvenience might befall the body. Believe me, my Father, and if I am long, forgive me, that the mere memory consoles me, and hope recreates me; for: Ille terrarum mihi praeter omnes angulus ridet (that corner of the world smiles on me above all others) — “That corner of the earth smiles on me beyond all others” — as said the Venusian Poet. Honey, yes, truly honey — of celestial consolations, which yield neither to nectar and ambrosia, nor to all the delights of all time, nor even to the spiritual tastes which the persons of our Society continually experience in their chambers in these parts — of which our Father Xavier gives most clear testimony.",
   "It is a sweet thing to think, to speak, and even to write of these things, particularly to him from whom, after God and the aid of the saints, I await the execution, as I hope, and have some pledge, or earnest, of it; but I would not abuse the gentleness, courtesy, and charity of Your Paternity; therefore I close, humbly making you reverence, desirous of being able to pray for you from atop a cross. Amen."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Servant and most unworthy son in the Lord — Francesco Gargano. From Nola, July 25, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gargano, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Nola",
  "date_raw": "Nola, July 25, 1618",
  "date_only": "July 25, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 25,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 73,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2792"
 },
 "48": {
  "number": 48,
  "byline_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio — Mantua, August 23, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 70",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I write to Your Paternity not to disturb you with useless letters, but because a convenient occasion has offered itself, of one of ours who comes from Your Paternity, and also because His Divine Majesty, with ever greater impulses from day to day, shakes me ever more, that I ask this anew of Your Paternity. Your Paternity sees this to be the will of our Lord God; and the Father Provincial also desired to see this, who discerned that it was truly the will of God.",
   "I await also that Your Paternity may wish, and may explain to me his will — for my desire is so great to go either to China, or Japan, or the Indies, or Brazil, etc., that there is no hour, though I am most occupied, in which I do not remember it and say: “When will the Reverend Father General give me the license!”",
   "Your Paternity, I pray, by the passion of Jesus Christ, give this comfort to an unworthy brother of yours, who, moved by God, seeks to put into execution that which Your Paternity recommends to us in that letter of yours; and for this, trusting first in God, then in that great desire which Your Paternity shows in that letter, that we go to fish for souls in those countries, I await the license (there being so convenient an occasion at present) to be able to go with that Father to Brazil, that there I may put into execution what His Divine Majesty urges me to with frequent promptings; and in this way draw into the net of God, with the weak instrument of myself, many souls which God has reserved for me.",
   "Therefore let not Your Paternity leave me so afflicted, but consoled with giving it to me soon — for that end, as I have said — and also that I may prepare those books, etc., concerning which I would like to know whether Your Paternity is content that I begin to find them, that I may arrange to be more prepared. In this too let not Your Paternity abandon me, but see to it that in this that province may remain accommodated and satisfied. I shall not torment you more, but [I shall close] by asking anew that Your Paternity give me such license. Be well."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most unworthy Brother — Antonio Rondonio. From Mantua, August 23, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Mantua",
  "date_raw": "Mantua, August 23, 1618",
  "date_only": "August 23, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 8,
  "day": 23,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 75,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2771"
 },
 "49": {
  "number": 49,
  "byline_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio — Modena, October 30, 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 88",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Your Paternity will perhaps accuse me because I am too importunate in writing to you; but know that until you have fulfilled my desire to go to the Indies, etc., you will always mark me for this. To tell the truth, I can find rest neither day nor night, for I always have my thought fixed, meditating in this fashion: when will Your Paternity give me that so-desired license to go to the Indies, that there I may employ my forces and my life in the salvation of those souls; and in this fashion awaiting the desired rest, which will bring me rest while I labor much. And thinking that it had arrived, when there arrived at Modena those two Fathers, one of whom was going to Portugal, the other to Japan, whom I should accompany, I stood all glad at seeing them; but then, on hearing there was no news of me, although I conformed myself to the will of God — in all things a consoler of adversity — nonetheless I could not help being somewhat saddened.",
   "In Your Paternity I trust that as soon as possible you will show me the course in which, running and laboring for our Lord God, I shall find rest; and I pray you, for the love of the Most Blessed Virgin Mary, if anything avails with Your Paternity, and for the love of Christ, who stimulates me to so great a work — for so it pleases Him — give me as soon as possible license to depart to some part of the world, to the gaining of souls for Heaven, whether in Japan, China, Brazil, etc. And I, meanwhile, while I wait, shall attend to my school, which the Father Provincial has given me in Modena."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most unworthy servant and brother — Antonio Rondonio. Modena, October 30, 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Rondonio, Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Modena",
  "date_raw": "Modena, October 30, 1618",
  "date_only": "October 30, 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 30,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 77,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2772"
 },
 "50": {
  "number": 50,
  "byline_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo — Palermo, December 1618",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 98",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Although the seeing of our companions called to go and cultivate the field of the Lord in the Indies, and our being left behind, bring us a good and holy envy, and an inconsolable desire to be their companions — nevertheless this same gives us hope that one day the same may befall us, with as much gladness as we discern in them; and the pain of our delayed calling is dulled by thinking of the boundless content the Lord will grant us, when we shall set out to those parts, where now we see our companions make the way for us by preceding us. May it please the Lord that such a happy day be near!",
   "And may Your Paternity — by means of whom, in doing this, the Lord God makes use — for the bowels of the charity of Christ, shorten the days and call us soon, not looking at the unfitness of such a subject, but trusting from the Lord every help and grace for that to which He calls us. Nothing more. For the rest, I commend myself to your holy prayers and sacrifices — I commend myself greatly."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most humble servant in Christ — Vincenzo Fassari. From Palermo, December 1618."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Fassari, Vincenzo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, December 1618",
  "date_only": "December 1618",
  "year": 1618,
  "month": 12,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 79,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2821"
 },
 "51": {
  "number": 51,
  "byline_raw": "Gargano, Francesco — Naples, January 3, 1619",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 736, 2",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Considering this heroic action which the Son of God performed eight days after His Nativity, my heart grows tender, and for two reasons which move me to supplicate Your Paternity and to seek from you a most rich offering — what we call an inferta — I also feel driven to render to this most sweet and most loving Child some sort of return. One reason is that, if He, so early, begins to suffer and shed His blood for my sins, I who am the guilty one ought not to remain idle, not only to seek to mortify myself but also to give, or at least to expose, my life for love of Him, according to my vocation. The other reason is that the Child takes the name of Jesus, and sheds His blood, while I most unworthily am to call myself a member of the Company of Jesus, without shedding my blood for so precious a name given to me by Jesus Himself out of His infinite goodness — certainly not; but with divine grace I shall ever strive to answer my vocation, the outcome of which I humbly and suppliantly seek from Your Paternity. I add to all this that, since so many called to this enterprise have so fully answered it, it does not seem fitting to me, nor do I satisfy my vocation, unless I burn with desire to put it into execution; I hope to do so exactly, and shall ever strive more and more to prepare myself for the enterprise with the favor of the Blessed Virgin, to whom long ago I commended this matter, which I know most evidently to have come from God, for His greater glory and my perfection. So I finish, and, as I said, this offering I seek from Your Paternity by the blood of the Child Jesus, and by the love He shows us; and I assure Your Paternity that, if I obtain this prize, I shall be rich, and nothing else shall be lacking to me, and there shall be nothing left for me to desire but that — trusting in the goodness of God — I may yet hope to pray for Your Paternity above a cross, or amid irons, or in some other sort of torments; but meanwhile I promise not to fail to provide myself with the grace of God, and may Your Paternity remember this poor wretch. So I pray to God to give you a most happy new year, a blessed Easter, and every height of spiritual happiness; and together with Baldassarro [—] I commend myself to Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy servant in the Lord — Francesco Gargano. From Naples, January 3, 1619."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gargano, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, January 3, 1619",
  "date_only": "January 3, 1619",
  "year": 1619,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 3,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 80,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2796"
 },
 "52": {
  "number": 52,
  "byline_raw": "Barletta, Francesco — Naples, August 3, 1619",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 736, 53",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I wrote to Your Paternity in months past that I wished to go to Our Lady of Loreto, and I ask whether Your Paternity would grant me this grace: to come barefoot and on foot, and to come by way of Rome to see Your Paternity; but if I come, there shall happen what happened to Jacob, who wrestled a whole night with an Angel — so shall I do with Your Paternity, except that, prostrate upon the ground, I shall never leave Your Paternity until you grant me what I crave and have craved, as Your Paternity knows, though I know myself unworthy of such a grace. Yet I ask this by the merits of our Blessed Father Ignatius and of Blessed Xavier — apropos of those parts whither I wish to go — and, so as to trouble Your Paternity no more, I promise to give you no more trouble, for I hope that with this letter Your Paternity will console me, if you are willing; but in this let not my will be done, but God's, through Your Paternity; and so as to weary Your Paternity no more I make an end, commending myself to your Holy Sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy son of the Society, and of Your Paternity — Francesco Barletta. From Naples, August 3, 1619."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Barletta, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, August 3, 1619",
  "date_only": "August 3, 1619",
  "year": 1619,
  "month": 8,
  "day": 3,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 82,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2765"
 },
 "53": {
  "number": 53,
  "byline_raw": "Barletta, Francesco — Barletta, May 16, 1620",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 736, 153",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "It is proper to the son to ask of his father, as I have done many times with Your Paternity, my most dear Father — that is, asking many times to be sent to those parts of the Indies for the greater service of God. Though I know myself most unworthy of such a grace on account of my faults and defects, I hope nevertheless in the goodness and mercy of my Lord, who will inspire you in these days of the coming of the Holy Father, that you may fulfill my desire. I find myself in Barletta to this end: to detach myself from my mother, that I may be more ready, as Your Paternity wrote me to do; do with me as you please. Nothing more; I commend myself most to your holy sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's servant in Christ — Francesco Barletta. Barletta, May 16, 1620."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Barletta, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Barletta",
  "date_raw": "Barletta, May 16, 1620",
  "date_only": "May 16, 1620",
  "year": 1620,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 16,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 83,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2767"
 },
 "54": {
  "number": 54,
  "byline_raw": "Gargano, Francesco — Naples, May 29, 1620",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 736, 156",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "My hopes — strengthened by the encouragement I have on many occasions received from Your Paternity — of sailing, for the love of Christ who calls me to it, to the farthest parts of the world, make me think that, if my sins do not oppose it, on this present occasion they might be fulfilled. May they be fulfilled through the mercies of Jesus, and by the abundant blood He lavished for me; and by such liberality may it come about that I too show myself liberal with my own. [...] This sole consolation I desire, and I well know that long waiting ought to count as nothing in order to acquire what I desire, [...] ... Commending myself to the paternal charity of the Most Blessed [Mary], I remain with sounder and more living hopes, and I make Your Paternity most humble reverence."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy and most humble servant in the Lord — Francesco Gargano. From Naples, May 29, 1620."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gargano, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, May 29, 1620",
  "date_only": "May 29, 1620",
  "year": 1620,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 29,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 84,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2797"
 },
 "55": {
  "number": 55,
  "byline_raw": "Stella, Marco — Palermo, June 9, 1620",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 736, 161",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "On the occasion of the feast of the Holy Spirit I could not do otherwise than manifest to Your Paternity the flame — not wholly hidden from you — which, with the coming of this divine fire sent from Heaven, has grown in my heart: I mean the desire for the mission of the Indies. Believe me, Your Paternity, I have no words to describe it to you. May this Divine Spirit — who has so kindled me with desire that He seems to let me think of nothing else but this — who is at once tongue and pen, according to that Lingua mea calamus scribæ (my tongue is the pen of a scribe), explain it to you and write it in your heart, so that you, through the charity you bear toward your own, to the greater glory of God may deign to console me in this year, the last of my studies of philosophy, as I hope you will, trusting in the mercy of this Divine Spirit who spurs me to such a vocation, appeased by the prayers of the Most Blessed Virgin Mary, my advocate in all things as in this particular matter, for love of whom, with reverence, I beg Your Paternity to delay no longer in consoling this your servant, who can admit no consolation but this. To your prayers and Holy sacrifices I commend myself greatly."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant in the Lord — Marco Stella. From Palermo, June 9, 1620."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Stella, Marco",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, June 9, 1620",
  "date_only": "June 9, 1620",
  "year": 1620,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 85,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2808"
 },
 "56": {
  "number": 56,
  "byline_raw": "Gargano, Francesco — Salerno, September 30, 1621",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 736, 288",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Although, as I have always done, so also now I place myself in the hands of Your Paternity concerning going to the East Indies or else to the West, nevertheless — as I have also sometimes signified to Your Paternity — I have the greater desire, in the hope there is of shedding blood for love of Jesus, to go to the Eastern Indies; whence, having heard some time ago that Father Laerzio would soon be in Rome — [thinking] that he is already there — I have wished anew, with the resignation [...], to renew before Your Paternity my desires, reminding you that, my philosophy being finished, with the time of the voyage and a little more besides, I could, once sent there, employ myself in the salvation of those peoples. I stand at the door, and knock. Your Paternity well knows I am in good bodily health, thanks be to the Lord, who will doubtless supply my other failings. So, everything placed in the hands of Your Paternity, I make you humble reverence."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Francesco Gargano. From Salerno, September 30, 1621."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gargano, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Salerno",
  "date_raw": "Salerno, September 30, 1621",
  "date_only": "September 30, 1621",
  "year": 1621,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 30,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 86,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2798"
 },
 "57": {
  "number": 57,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, January 26, 1624",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 737, 94",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "It is already three years since His Divine Majesty deigned to give me the vocation to enter the Society, and from that time He also gave me another, of no lesser worth — that of going to the Indies, to convert those souls redeemed with the blood of Christ our Lord; driven by which I come to supplicate Your Paternity, for love of that same Christ, to will to put this my desire into effect. I write you nothing more, for I know that for your great charity you will do me this grace."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, most unworthy servant in Christ — Leonardo Cinami. From Naples, January 26, 1624."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, January 26, 1624",
  "date_only": "January 26, 1624",
  "year": 1624,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 26,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 87,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2841"
 },
 "58": {
  "number": 58,
  "byline_raw": "Stella, Marco — Caltagirone, April 9, 1624",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 737, 105",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The letter which Your Paternity deigned to send me in reply to mine has produced in me two very different effects: the first of greatest and exceeding joy, both for recognizing in those few lines the heart of my most loving Father, and for the hope it gives me of being consoled. But amid such joy and jubilation of my heart I cannot but confess that there is mingled an incredible sadness — which is the second effect — seeing that for my innumerable sins and ingratitude toward my sweetest Lord, to which all this must be attributed, the sum of my desires is deferred to me; and this sadness has lately received greater increase, seeing that Your Paternity now calls others, and not me, to this most blessed Mission. And although I know there is not in me that merit which these servants of the Lord have, to be thus consoled, nevertheless, spurred by the desire and the flame I have within my breast, I have had the boldness to unburden my heart to Your Paternity and to pray you with all possible affection, in visceribus Iesu Christi, et per merita Beatae Verginis Matris meæ (in the heart of Jesus Christ, and through the merits of the Blessed Virgin my Mother), to deign to console me this once together with the others. And know — which I tell you sincerely as to the father of my soul — that I feel this flame grow so much in me that I would go even on foot, if it were permitted me. And if this were too great an exaggeration for Your Paternity to believe, be assured at least of this: that I offer myself most willingly (especially if it is not now convenient to multiply the number of missionaries) as a servant-boy to them, to serve them and carry their baggage; and so I promise you in the sight of the Lord, whom I pray not to regard my strength and ability, which are nothing for such an enterprise, but His great mercy, and to choose me through Your Paternity, as He has deigned to communicate to me this so fervent desire for His greater glory. I would add many other words, if I did not fear to weary you; but I am silent on them — the Most Blessed Virgin, my [principal] advocate in this matter, will speak for me in the heart of Your Paternity. To your Holy sacrifices and prayers I commend myself greatly."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant in the Lord — Marco Stella. Caltagirone, April 9, 1624."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Stella, Marco",
  "place_raw": "Caltagirone",
  "date_raw": "Caltagirone, April 9, 1624",
  "date_only": "April 9, 1624",
  "year": 1624,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 88,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2809"
 },
 "59": {
  "number": 59,
  "byline_raw": "Stella, Marco — Caltagirone, April 15, 1624",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 737, 106",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "On the occasion of the departure for Rome of Master Giovanni Scorso, my classmate, summoned by Your Paternity — as is believed, for the Indies — I have not wished to fail to write again to Your Paternity (as some days ago I wrote) and to lay before you my desire for the most blessed Mission of the Indies with such warmth, as if I had never before laid it before you. And if these my so frequent and importunate letters should give you trouble, I pray Your Paternity, for love of the good Jesus, to excuse me; for vim patior (I suffer violence). And oh, what sweet and loving violence — which will not let me, nor shall let me, rest at all until I am by Your Paternity, according to my desire, consoled! What would you have me say? Or rather, what can I say of the greatness and vehemence of this my desire? Oh that the Lord, who communicated it to me, would deign with His divine and enkindled tongue to speak in the heart of Your Paternity; and He who can would explain to you what and how great is the flame that inwardly consumes me. Oh how surely I would count myself to be soon consoled! What shall I say of the ardent longing to shed my blood for Him who first shed it for me in love on a hard Cross? What of the zeal for so many souls that are lost, which break and melt my heart? What of the desire for my salvation, which by this means alone, inspired by God, I seem to have to attain? And in sum, all good seems to me to depend on this alone, and on one word which I await from Your Paternity — if my sins do not hinder it — and that word is Veni (come). O sweet word, and to my ears most welcome, for which I would go even on foot all the way to the Indies, to follow my sweet Jesus, who sweetly invites me and comforts me with solid hope. I shall say no more; Master Giovanni Scorso will be able to tell Your Paternity the rest. It will now touch Your Paternity (as in Domino (in the Lord) he shall judge) to console me as soon as possible, as in the name of the Lord and of His Most Holy Mother I supplicate you. To the most holy sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I commend myself from my heart."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Of Your Paternity, servant in the Lord — Marco Stella. Caltagirone, April 15, 1624."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Stella, Marco",
  "place_raw": "Caltagirone",
  "date_raw": "Caltagirone, April 15, 1624",
  "date_only": "April 15, 1624",
  "year": 1624,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 15,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 90,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2810"
 },
 "60": {
  "number": 60,
  "byline_raw": "Gargano, Francesco — December 5, 1624",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 737, 222",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "At the news that has come here of the good harvest prepared in Ethiopia, where two hundred of ours are called, my ancient desire has been kindled further, and I have entered into hope that into that numerous [company] there may [enter] one most lowly and unworthy of the name and title of soldier of Christ. My Father, remember me for love of God, and do not look at my little being, but at the infinite power of Him who called me and keeps me in this holy desire, and who delights and glories in raising up the needy from the earth [inopem (a poor one)]. The desire is matured, unless I err; the call is from God; the execution rests with God, entrusted — as the least interpreter, guided by the same God — to Your Paternity, to whom I make humble reverence, praying you to give me the Holy blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Unworthy servant and son [most ...] in the Lord — Francesco Gargano. December 5, 1624."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Gargano, Francesco",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "December 5, 1624",
  "date_only": "December 5, 1624",
  "year": 1624,
  "month": 12,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 92,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2794"
 },
 "61": {
  "number": 61,
  "byline_raw": "Stella, Marco — Messina, December 8, 1625",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 737, 334",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Although I wrote to Your Paternity a few days ago with Father Giacomo Domenichi, Procurator of this Province, and that letter seemed as though it would have sufficed to remind Your Paternity of the fiery desire which, by His goodness, Our Lord Jesus Christ has communicated to me for the most blessed Mission of the Indies, nevertheless so great is the fire, which grows greater in my heart every day, that — though I scarcely doubted I would weary you with my importunate letters — it has nonetheless driven me, even against my will, to write to you anew, and anew, with affectionate tears, by the wounds of the Good Jesus, to supplicate you that, if the salvation of my soul is dear to you — as your most ardent charity as a Father promises me — you will console me in a matter so closely joined to it. O my sweetest Father, I feel an interior voice telling me that now is the time. O happy my soul, if I shall have it, on such an occasion, for love of the blessed Jesus, to lose — or rather, to gain.",
   "O what thirst my Lord communicates to me to shed my blood and my life for those souls for whom the same God did not spare His own! I pray for nothing else in my cold prayers each day to the Lord; I desire nothing else. And especially today, which is the day of the Immaculate Conception of the Most Holy Virgin — my particular Lady and guide, above all in this matter — I cannot say how greatly my desire has grown, so that I seem unable to endure life any longer without its fulfillment. And if Your Paternity doubts my ability, you act as a prudent Shepherd; and I too confess myself most unfit and unworthy. But, O Father of my soul, I beg you, for love of the Blessed Virgin, no longer lower your eyes upon my baseness and unfitness, but rather raise them to behold the one who calls — who can not only give me every fitness, but, by His great goodness, as I have most certain hope, will give it to me. Behold Jesus, who draws me to Himself by such a path, and do not discourage me with long waiting. And if ever there was a time when I asked you this grace with fervor, this is that time, in which, with the coming of the Father Procurator of the Indies, many will be called to those blessed kingdoms — among whom I pray as affectionately as I can that Your Paternity will number me as the least of all, and, if for no other reason, at least to serve them on the voyage as a ship's boy; and to this, with the greatest sincerity of my heart, in penance for my grave sins and for love of my Good Jesus, I dedicate myself."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Most unworthy servant in the Lord — Marco Stella. Messina, December 8, 1625."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Stella, Marco",
  "place_raw": "Messina",
  "date_raw": "Messina, December 8, 1625",
  "date_only": "December 8, 1625",
  "year": 1625,
  "month": 12,
  "day": 8,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 93,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2811"
 },
 "62": {
  "number": 62,
  "byline_raw": "Balami, Ignazio — Messina, January 15, 1626",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 735, 316",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "With this occasion of the coming of Father Basile, our minister of the College, I have not wished to fail in greeting Your Paternity, so that even in this way the great ardor and desire of my heart may be somewhat vented: to see myself employed, as I wrote to Your Paternity, in the harvest of souls in the farthest parts of the world, so that by this means I may do some little thing pleasing in the service of His Divine Majesty, so often and so grievously offended by me. And Your Paternity should know that no other motive is found in me for this but the desire to begin to satisfy God, by this path, for the multitude of my sins — which in no other way could so effectively succeed for me but by this act of exposing myself, putting my life at stake in the farthest parts of the world, for Him who for my love died upon a hard wood of the Cross.",
   "Father, Your Paternity should know that this desire has so pierced my heart that of nothing else would I think, of nothing else would I speak; indeed in nothing else does it find rest but in this. And so I pray Your Paternity to look upon me with the eyes with which Christ on the Cross regarded the good thief when He said to him, hodie mecum eris in Paradiso (today thou shalt be with me in paradise) — and that Your Paternity do the same to me, calling me, so that I may be one of those holy men who are sent forth for the Kingdom of Paradise.",
   "By divine grace I am sound and whole; by this path I hope, by the Divine mercy, to make myself holy, as I indicated to Your Paternity in another letter, of which, if it please Your Paternity, you will receive information from the same Father Basile. I finish, so as not to weary you further, by humbly asking the blessing of Your Paternity, praying to heaven for every grace from the Lord for you — though unworthily — and Your Paternity should know that I am precisely a soul in Purgatory, awaiting the Angel to bear me to heaven; and for me that Angel will be Your Paternity, admitting me to such dignity as to be one of those who go to die for their God. And I shall never forget Your Paternity, so that the Lord may lavishly give you every grace from heaven."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's son who wishes you every good — Ignazio Balami. From Messina, January 15, 1626."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Balami, Ignazio",
  "place_raw": "Messina",
  "date_raw": "Messina, January 15, 1626",
  "date_only": "January 15, 1626",
  "year": 1626,
  "month": 1,
  "day": 15,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 95,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2833"
 },
 "63": {
  "number": 63,
  "byline_raw": "Stella, Marco — Messina, June 7, 1626",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 737, 395",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I know well — and in the past I have always known — the unfitness I bear for so lofty a Vocation as this of the Indies; and yet the greatness of my desire emboldens me to supplicate Your Paternity anew for it. And now more than ever I trust in the Lord, since there is so good an opportunity in the coming of the Father Procurator from those parts, to enjoy the fruit of so long a waiting. Your Paternity, for love of the Lord, remember this unworthy slave of the Holy Society, and admit me to the number of those chosen for this Holy Mission.",
   "O my Father, beneath the blessed Jesus, with how many tears do I write this! Oh, may it please the Most Holy Trinity — whose feast we celebrate today — that, as this letter comes sprinkled with tears, so it may return to me cum exultatione portans manipulos suos (bearing his sheaves with exultation), with the happy effect. I pray Your Paternity not to allow that my desire remain longer fasting and empty of its execution — fedeche ecce iam decennio (behold, these ten years now), I can say, and non triduo sustineo Dominum (not for three days do I await the Lord). For as you well remember, since I was a Novice I have had this desire, and it has always grown ever more, as you will have been able to know from my letters. But what do I say — from the Novitiate? When I was still a child, in the darkness of Egypt, the Lord communicated this desire to me; indeed this was the sole motive for which I entered the Most Holy Society.",
   "Therefore, O my Father, with the greatest humility I pray you anew, for love of the Blessed Virgin, not to defer the execution of my petition any longer, so that I may follow the naked Jesus in contempt of myself and of my life for all the rest of this my pilgrimage; for as the Lord has given me grace to know my ingratitude toward His Divine Majesty — as for some time now, with extraordinary interior renewal, I have experienced — so too He gives me a most ardent desire to employ myself and my life in His service usque ad mortem (even unto death), a desire which may make known to Your Paternity His most holy Will, and perfectly fulfill it. With which I commend myself to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy servant in the Lord — The least of the whole Society, Marco Stella. Messina, June 7, 1626."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Stella, Marco",
  "place_raw": "Messina",
  "date_raw": "Messina, June 7, 1626",
  "date_only": "June 7, 1626",
  "year": 1626,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 7,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 97,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2812"
 },
 "64": {
  "number": 64,
  "byline_raw": "Bartoli, Daniello (1608-1685) — Parma, February 2, 1627",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 738, 7",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The vehement desires which, by His infinite goodness, the Lord God deigns to communicate to me — to amplify as far as possible throughout the whole World His glory, spending, after every sort of fatigue and hardship, even my life amid the greatest torments — compel me to have recourse to Your Paternity, in whose hands, after the Lord God, lies the granting me their fulfillment. I confess coram Domino (before the Lord) to Your Paternity that, by the grace which His Divine Majesty at times grants me of knowing some particle of what His Divine being merits, all the glory which His Saints have hitherto rendered Him seems to me nothing in comparison with that which it gives me the desire to procure for Him. And for this, though indifferent, yet with the greatest affection I offer myself at his feet as the most unworthy instrument of the Divine Glory in the conversion of the Infidels, in whatever part of the World Your Paternity shall judge to send me — desirous, whatever I may be, of enduring to this end every sort of suffering, and, if it please the Lord God, death.",
   "Nor does the clear knowledge and continual experience of my very little spirit and total unfitness for so great an enterprise at all draw me back from asking so great a thing: rather, just as for three years it persuaded me not to dare to disclose it to Your Paternity, so now it emboldens me all the more, knowing the custom of the Lord God, who at times is wont to choose for the greatest enterprises those least worthy of them. Cast therefore at your feet, my most loving Father, with all possible affection I offer myself to you anew — wholly indifferent in all things, and in all things eager to apply myself as soon as possible to the labors of the Indies, and to the desired end of the Divine Glory in Martyrdom. And in conclusion, praying Him for you from heaven every fullness of the Holy Spirit, I humbly ask, prostrate, your paternal blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy son and servant — Daniele Bartoli. Parma, February 2, 1627."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Bartoli, Daniello (1608-1685)",
  "place_raw": "Parma",
  "date_raw": "Parma, February 2, 1627",
  "date_only": "February 2, 1627",
  "year": 1627,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 2,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 99,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2859"
 },
 "65": {
  "number": 65,
  "byline_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672) — July 1627",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 738, 126",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Having heard that whoever desires to go to the Indies should now, there being a convenient occasion and need of men, make it known by giving a memorial — though in me I am certain (as my Superior can well know) that I have neither the virtue nor the qualities requisite for going there — nevertheless, both because I trust that, if I were sent, the strength of holy obedience would supply the lack, and also because I am convinced that whoever has some desire should manifest it: I manifest to Your Most Reverend Paternity that I would most willingly go both to the Indies and to any other mission whatsoever, from a desire both to detach myself from the sight of kinsfolk and country — other attachments toward which things, it seems to me, are not yet dead in me — and of Saint Andrew's, to which I am in some way attached, and from a desire to aid souls, according to my ability, in conformity with my vocation.",
   "Wherefore I pray Your Most Reverend Paternity that, if you judge that the greater honor and glory of God — with all my imperfections and insufficiencies presupposed — lies in detaching me from here, where I willingly remain, you deign to grant it to me, offering myself therefore to do as and how Your Most Reverend Paternity shall order."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Most Reverend Paternity's most obedient son and servant in Christ — Francesco Bressani."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672)",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "July 1627",
  "date_only": "July 1627",
  "year": 1627,
  "month": 7,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 101,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2729"
 },
 "66": {
  "number": 66,
  "byline_raw": "Bartoli, Daniello (1608-1685) — Parma, February 1, 1628",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 738, 189",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Behold me cast once more at your feet by the most ardent desire which, by His mercy, the Lord God gives me to amplify His Holy Name in aiding the infidels, and amid the greatest hardships and sufferings of persecutions. O if, my most loving Father, I might find a place in the first occasions of departing toward harvests and treasures so precious! I feel great confidence in saying it, asking it of you as the first grace, to the glory of these our newly blessed Martyr Brothers: speak, Your Paternity, and in their name grant it to me. My most loving Father, the infinity of glory which that incomprehensible being of the Lord God merits — the sole motive of this desire of mine, had from the time I knew Him — would require of me infinite years and infinite work: ah, for His love do not deny me at least, Your Paternity, these my first [years], which I will spend, oh how willingly and wholeheartedly, in whatever service of those who occupy themselves in so precious and Divine an office as the conversion of the Gentiles.",
   "There is England, Japan, China, and so many other barbarous and heretical lands: may I too, least of all who ask you such a grace, find in so great a vastness some place where, according to my desire, I may spend my life in the conversion of souls and finish it in the fire. To obtain this, cast prostrate at his feet, I ask your holy blessing, awaiting as soon as possible the fruit of it."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Most Reverend Paternity's most unworthy Servant and Son in the Lord — Daniele Bartoli. Parma, February 1, 1628."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Bartoli, Daniello (1608-1685)",
  "place_raw": "Parma",
  "date_raw": "Parma, February 1, 1628",
  "date_only": "February 1, 1628",
  "year": 1628,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 1,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 102,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2860"
 },
 "67": {
  "number": 67,
  "byline_raw": "Vimercati, Orazio — Genoa, July 24, 1628",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 738, 246",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "My having up to now not manifested to Your Paternity my Vocation for the Indies was not because God has newly sent it to me — for since I asked to enter the Society I always asked with this intention — but by counsel of my Father Rector of the Novitiate, who told me I ought first to apply myself to grounding myself well in virtue. Now at last, though unworthy, having been admitted to the first degree of the Society, and finding myself at the beginning of my studies — a Milanese by country, aged nineteen, a Humanist of two years in Letters — I have resolved, with the counsel of my Superiors, to disclose to Your Paternity that I truly desire to spend my life in whatever part of the Indies, and above all in that one where, for the completion of my labors, I might for reward shed my blood for Him who so lavishly shed His for me on the Cross.",
   "And this I have always desired, and have always asked of the Lord with great insistence in my prayers, and I feel I have from God strength, both spiritual and bodily, to endure whatever fatigue and hardship. Hoping then to obtain so great a grace, I come with this my [letter] humbly to offer myself to Your Paternity with all largeness of heart and readiness of mind, suppliantly asking that you deign to fulfill this desire of mine as soon as possible, to the greater glory of God and my own good; with which I make an end, making you humble reverence and commending myself to your Holy Sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most humble servant and unworthy son — Horatio",
   "Vimercato of the Society of Jesus. From Genoa, July 24, 1628."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Vimercati, Orazio",
  "place_raw": "Genoa",
  "date_raw": "Genoa, July 24, 1628",
  "date_only": "July 24, 1628",
  "year": 1628,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 24,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 103,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2705"
 },
 "68": {
  "number": 68,
  "byline_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672) — September 1628",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 738, 286",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I would not have had the boldness — considering my natural, spiritual, and moral unfitnesses, of weak health and little or no virtue — to propose to Your Most Reverend Paternity the desire which Our Lord God, on the occasion of reading the Life of the glorious Saint Francis Xavier, deigned to communicate to me, of the Mission to the Indies, if on the other hand, the interior Reasons which made this fulfillment difficult for me having been overcome by divine mercy, I had not known the obligation of corresponding to the divine Inspirations, and had not seen how God at times takes pleasure, to show His Omnipotence and goodness more, and for His other secret Judgments, in making use, for the aid of souls, of the weak and feeble, and those at times esteemed of no worth.",
   "Wherefore, representing to Your Most Reverend Paternity this desire of mine just as it is, with the present occasions, I pray that, if you judge the execution of it the greater service of His Divine Majesty, you do not fail to grant it to me, trusting in His Divine Majesty, who facit ea quae non sunt tamquam ea quae sunt (who makes the things that are not as though they were), to overcome all the difficulties that might oppose me in this. And with this, asking your holy blessing, I pray His Divine Majesty to deign to inspire in you what is to His greater Glory."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Most Reverend Paternity's most humble son and Servant in Christ — Francesco Giuseppe Bressani."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672)",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "September 1628",
  "date_only": "September 1628",
  "year": 1628,
  "month": 9,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 105,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2730"
 },
 "69": {
  "number": 69,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, September 9, 1628",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 738, 274",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "A long time ago I wrote to Your Paternity to remember me on occasions of sending men to the New World, to toil in something for the conversion of those peoples; I return now with this to remind you of the same, to manifest to you anew my desires in this regard, which grow greater in me day by day. Fulfill them for love of God at last, Your Paternity, and do not look upon my little ability and my little fervor, which seem to render me unworthy of so many graces; for I too hope — trusting, however, in the strength of my Lord — to be able to bow my neck to naked swords and open my breast to lances when need be, as so many of His sons continually do with so much glory.",
